@page "/docs/concepts/oauth-flow"
@layout DocsLayout
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

<PageTitle>OAuth 2.0 + PKCE — Vaultex</PageTitle>

<div class="docs-eyebrow">Concepts</div>
<h1>OAuth 2.0 + PKCE</h1>
<p class="docs-lead">
    The exact protocol Vaultex uses to authenticate users and issue tokens. This page walks through
    every step of the Authorization Code flow with PKCE, including the request and response shapes.
</p>

<h2 id="why-pkce">Why PKCE?</h2>
<p>
    The classic OAuth 2.0 Authorization Code flow assumes the client has a secret it can use to
    prove its identity when exchanging a code for tokens. That works for backends, but a SPA or
    mobile app can't keep a secret &mdash; anyone can read the JavaScript or decompile the binary.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>PKCE</strong> (Proof Key for Code Exchange) replaces the static secret with a one-time
    challenge generated for each sign-in. The client invents a random string (the <em>verifier</em>),
    hashes it (the <em>challenge</em>), and sends only the hash with the initial request. When it
    later exchanges the code for tokens, it sends the original verifier. Vaultex hashes it and
    compares &mdash; if they match, the request is legit.
</p>
<p>
    Vaultex requires PKCE for <em>all</em> clients, including confidential ones. There's no downside
    to defense in depth.
</p>

<h2 id="step-by-step">The flow, step by step</h2>

<h3>1. Generate a code verifier and challenge</h3>
<p>The client picks 43&ndash;128 random characters and SHA-256 hashes them.</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">JavaScript</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>const verifier  = base64url(crypto.getRandomValues(new Uint8Array(32)));
const challenge = base64url(await sha256(verifier));
sessionStorage.setItem("pkce_verifier", verifier);</code></pre>
</div>

<h3>2. Send the user to the authorize endpoint</h3>
<p>Redirect the browser to Vaultex with the challenge attached.</p>

<div class="docs-endpoint">
    <span class="docs-method docs-method--get">GET</span>
    <span>/connect/authorize</span>
</div>

<table>
    <thead><tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>response_type</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Always <code>code</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>client_id</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>The <code>client_id</code> you registered.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>redirect_uri</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Must exactly match a registered redirect URI.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scope</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Space-separated list. <code>openid</code> is required.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>state</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Opaque value you'll get back unchanged. CSRF protection.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>code_challenge</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Base64URL-encoded SHA-256 of the verifier.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>code_challenge_method</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Always <code>S256</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>nonce</code></td><td>Recommended</td><td>Random value bound to the ID token. Replay protection.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h3>3. User signs in</h3>
<p>
    Vaultex shows the sign-in screen. Once the user authenticates (and consents, for first-time
    third-party clients), Vaultex redirects the browser back to your <code>redirect_uri</code>:
</p>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">HTTP</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>302 Found
Location: https://yourapp.com/callback?code=Abc123...&amp;state=xyz</code></pre>
</div>

<h3>4. Exchange the code for tokens</h3>
<p>The client posts back to the token endpoint with the verifier.</p>

<div class="docs-endpoint">
    <span class="docs-method docs-method--post">POST</span>
    <span>/connect/token</span>
</div>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">HTTP</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>POST /connect/token HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=authorization_code
&amp;code=Abc123...
&amp;redirect_uri=https://yourapp.com/callback
&amp;client_id=your-client-id
&amp;code_verifier=&lt;the original verifier&gt;</code></pre>
</div>

<h3>5. Receive the tokens</h3>

<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">JSON</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>{
  "access_token":  "eyJhbGciOi...",
  "id_token":      "eyJhbGciOi...",
  "refresh_token": "RT_8a3f...",
  "token_type":    "Bearer",
  "expires_in":    3600,
  "scope":         "openid profile email offline_access"
}</code></pre>
</div>

<h2 id="diagram">Visual reference</h2>
<div class="docs-codeblock">
    <div class="docs-codeblock-header">
        <span class="docs-codeblock-lang">Sequence</span>
        <button class="docs-codeblock-copy" onclick="vxCopy(this)">Copy</button>
    </div>
<pre><code>User              Your app              Vaultex
 │                  │                     │
 │── click sign in ▶│                     │
 │                  │── /authorize ─────▶ │
 │◄──── login UI ───┼─────────────────────│
 │── credentials ──┼──────────────────── ▶│
 │                  │◄── 302 + code ──────│
 │                  │── POST /token ────▶ │
 │                  │◄── access + id ─────│
 │◄── you're in ────│                     │</code></pre>
</div>

